# Grok Build Agent Client

Pontia runs Grok Build through the Grok CLI's ACP server. You can start a Grok Session on your device, follow its work from the Remote Dashboard, answer permission requests, interrupt it, and continue the same conversation later.

## Install Grok Build

Open **Settings → Agent Clients → Grok Build** and choose **Install**. Pontia uses xAI's official installer, verifies `grok --version`, and enables the Client. The installer supports Linux and macOS on x86-64 and Arm64 and requires Bash plus `curl` or `wget`.

To install from the device terminal instead, run:

```sh
pontia client install grok
```

If Grok is already installed by another method, make sure `grok` is on the Pontia service user's `PATH`, then enable it:

```sh
pontia client enable grok
```

Pontia starts each Session with `grok agent stdio` in its Workspace.

## Sign in

The first-client guide in the Remote Dashboard can start Grok's device-code login. Open the displayed authorization page on any device, enter the code, and return to the guide after authorization completes.

From a desktop terminal, you can also run:

```sh
grok login
```

On a headless or remote host, use the device flow directly:

```sh
grok login --device-auth
```

Run the command as the same operating-system user that runs Pontia. Grok also accepts `XAI_API_KEY` from the Pontia service environment. After changing the service environment or `PATH`, run `pontia up` so the managed service receives the new values.

## Control Grok remotely

In [New chat](https://app.pontia.dev/), select a Workspace, choose **Grok Build**, and select a model or the Client default. The Dashboard streams Grok's replies and work steps and shows permission requests for you to answer from another computer or phone.

While Grok is working, send another message to add instructions at its next tool or model transition. Grok continues the current Turn with the new instructions; when the last transition has already passed, Pontia delivers the message as a follow-up Turn. You can also interrupt the active Turn and then send the next request.

When the Session is idle or interrupted, choose **Compact context** or send `/compact`. Pontia keeps the Session open and adds the compaction checkpoint to its timeline.

## Choose a model and reasoning effort

Pontia reads Grok's model catalog for the selected Workspace. The model picker lists the available models and the reasoning-effort choices advertised for each model. Keeping **Client default** follows Grok's current default; when that model publishes effort levels, the same picker lets you choose one before starting the Session.

The Session header shows the effective model and reasoning effort reported by Grok.

## Use Grok for Bots, helpers, and Workflows

You can select Grok Build when creating a Bot. Bot Sessions, memory retrieval, and Dreaming use Pontia's restricted Grok profile, which provides file reading and terminal commands without approval prompts.

A Bot's general helper inherits the Bot's Grok model and reasoning effort. For a specialist helper, create an **Agent Profile** that selects Grok Build, a model, an optional reasoning effort, and its instructions. The Bot can then launch that profile with `pontia bot helper create --profile-id ID`.

The same Agent Profile can run a Workflow node. Set its ID in the Workflow definition:

```toml
[[nodes]]
type = "agent"
phase = "Review"
title = "Grok reviewer"
instructions = "Review the current changes and write the findings."
inputs = []
output = "review.md"
execution_profile_id = "grok-reviewer"
```

Enable **YOLO** on an Agent Profile when that helper or Workflow node should use Grok's usual tools without approval prompts. Ordinary API-created Sessions can select `default`, `yolo`, or `restricted` with the `mode` field described in [Agent Clients](/docs/agent-clients#client-credentials-and-permissions).

See [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting) if the executable is not detected or a Session does not start.
